#---
# purpose: "A dispatch arm is never seated ahead of a sibling holding elements its own surface does not name"
# oracle: oracle.exs
#---
#
# fz-kdt.129 / fz-kdt.131. All three calls reach one
# `List.reduce_while_step/3` callsite, which settles two arms:
#
#     arm A   list(:ok)  x  {Enum.all?/1 lambda, Enum.empty?/1 lambda}
#     arm B   list(int)  x  {Enum.all?/1 lambda}
#
# Arm B's runtime test used to be strictly INSIDE arm A's -- the same
# "a list" question, the same `:true` question, and a callable set of one
# against a set of two. Seat the smaller test first and `Enum.all?([:ok, :ok])`
# satisfied every question arm B asks, because a list test could not see
# elements, and landed in the body compiled for `list(int)`:
# `fz_list_head_int_ref` reads an atom as an int and aborts on `run` and
# `build`. `interp` printed the right answers anyway -- its values carry their
# own tags -- so only the two compiled doors showed it.
#
# Neither arm's surface covers the other's at the list position, so no seat was
# escape-free here and the seating rule declined to move the pair: it kept
# arrival order, which is what these three lines pin. The standing cure named
# then was a runtime test that can see a list's elements, and fz-kdt.107 step 3
# built it: the two arms ask `[:ok]` and `[int]` of a cons cell's HEAD, which
# are disjoint questions, so neither can take the other's values however the
# pair is seated. The lines below print the same answers -- what changed is
# that they are no longer safe by arrival. Measured at that landing: this
# fixture aborted on `run` and `build` under EVERY arm seed the fz-kdt.141
# stress produces, and now exits 0 under all of them.
#
# fz-kdt.131 still owns the population one rung wider: a pair whose heads
# OVERLAP while neither surface contains the other, which no seat can reach.
fn main() do
  dbg(Enum.all?([:ok, :ok]))
  dbg(Enum.empty?([:ok, :ok]))
  dbg(Enum.all?([1, 2]))
end
